#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build a proxy-residual pair table from a numerical ImPi source.

Theory:

    Pi_num = Pi_ref + DeltaPi_res
    Pi_ref = Pi_vac,cutoff + Pi_proxy,medium   (original proxy_residual mode)
    Pi_ref = Pi_QP-QP                          (qp_subtracted mode — UV-safe)
    Pi_ref = Pi_vac,cutoff                     (direct_vacuum_kk mode)
    Gamma_num^{-1} = Gamma_ref^{-1} - DeltaPi_res

The key point is that the KK reconstruction is applied only to the residual
DeltaPi_res, which must decay at large |omega|.

In the original proxy_residual mode Im(Pi_ref) → -1/8 at large Omega (vacuum
log with k→∞), while Im(Pi_SC) → 0 at Omega > 2*k_cut^2/m - mu_up - mu_dn,
leaving a permanent +1/8 offset in ImDeltaPi that breaks the KK.

The qp_subtracted mode avoids this: Im(Pi_QP-QP) is computed analytically from
per-k Dyson poles extracted from the SC cubes and decays as ~1/Omega^2 (finite
k_cut Lorentzian tails), so ImDeltaPi = Im(Pi_SC) - Im(Pi_QP-QP) → 0 by
construction for all q.

The direct_vacuum_kk mode is the minimal finite-cutoff reconstruction used by
the validated high-polarization candidate.  It Kramers-Kronig transforms only
Im(Pi_num-Pi_vac,cutoff) on the native omega grid and adds the exact complex
finite-cutoff vacuum bubble back.  It applies no residual taper and no proxy
or QP reference.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

